WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Good to have you on models and agents, episode 108 for July 11th, 2026.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.060
Your daily AI briefing.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.120
Let's see what happened in the AI world today, and trust me, it's been busy.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:22.180
GPT 5.6 just became the default model inside Microsoft 365 co-pilot, giving enterprise users

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.500
immediate access to its lower-cost reasoning without changing workflows.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:35.380
Open AI confirmed GPT 5.6 is now the preferred model across Microsoft 365 co-pilot.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.520
The same update also cut health intelligence costs dramatically, with GPT 5.6 Luna delivering

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:50.340
stronger results at 25 times lower cost than GPT 5.5 at its highest reasoning setting.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:57.860
This development sits inside the ongoing Frontier Dash models arc, tracked since episode 107,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.420
where closed labs continue trading capability and price advantages.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.840
Builders should watch how the new cost curve changes when they move existing co-pilot

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.920
automations onto the updated backend. The change routes, enterprise document,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.800
spreadsheet, and email tasks through the newer model without requiring users to select it manually.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.100
G-sheet 5.6 Luna variant outperforms the prior G-sheet 5.5 top reasoning tier,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.300
while running at 25 times lower cost, directly addressing the inference economics that have

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:39.960
limited large-scale co-pilot rollouts. Teams already licensed for Microsoft 365 gain the upgrade

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:46.080
immediately, shifting the build versus buy decision for internal agents that previously relied on

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:53.280
separate GPT calls. The move continues the pattern of open AI tightening integration with Microsoft

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:00.260
infrastructure, while lowering per token barriers for high-volume workloads. This fits the Frontier

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:06.080
models narrative from yesterday's coverage by extending the same cost reduction trajectory observed

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:13.900
across the GPT 5.6 family. Watch for similar default model swaps and other Microsoft surfaces,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:21.500
and whether the cost reduction extends to non-health verticals. GPT 5.6 Luna reaches higher

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:28.100
performance than GPT 5.5 at maximum reasoning effort while costing 25 times less.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:35.240
The update targets health intelligence workloads, but applies across the GPT 5.6 family.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:41.480
Builders running medical or compliance pipelines can now test the same quality tier at far

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:48.500
lower token budgets. The change also widens access for global users previously priced out of Frontier

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:54.700
reasoning. Open AI highlighted the improvement in a single post that paired the performance claim

00:02:54.700 --> 00:03:02.600
with the explicit 25 times multiplier. Open AI converted its biobug bounty into a standing private

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:10.600
program and doubled maximum rewards to $50,000. Researchers with red teaming or biosecurity experience

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:16.900
are invited to hunt for universal jail breaks against Frontier models on a predefined biosafety

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:23.140
challenge. The program remains invite only and focuses on constructive disclosure rather than

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:30.460
public exploits. The shift from a time-limited bounty to an ongoing program signals open AI's intent

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:37.640
to maintain continuous external pressure on biosafety boundaries. Teams working in regulated domains

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:43.800
should monitor the resulting safeguards for any new refusal patterns. A new proof of concept

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:51.040
called colibree demonstrates a 1.5 terabyte model executing on consumer hardware with only 25

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:57.480
gigabytes of RAM. The approach targets local AI setups and avoids cloud round trips for

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:03.460
sensitive workloads. Early results suggest the technique could let developers run large models

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:10.200
entirely on device without quantization. The memory reduction is achieved through a novel architectural

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.560
shortcut that keeps the full parameter set resident while streaming only active layers.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:22.020
The 25 gigabyte footprint was measured on a standard consumer GPU without any custom

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:29.100
quantization beyond the novel streaming technique. Fable 5 enforces a hard 15-word quote cap

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:34.880
from sources blocking longer verbatim excerpts even when they support factual claims.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:40.660
The restriction applies during research sessions where users expect direct citations.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:46.480
Simon Willison noted the limit is especially painful compared with more permissive models.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.300
The cap appears to stem from the same safety tuning that governs web search quoting rules,

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:58.920
creating an unintended side effect on uploaded documents. Developers relying on citation heavy

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:04.660
summarization should test alternative models or post-process outputs to restore context.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:11.360
Robinhood plans to enable AI agents to execute crypto trades on behalf of US customers.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:17.120
The feature extends the platform's existing agent tooling into live financial markets.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:23.360
No launch date or risk controls were detailed in the announcement. The move places Robinhood

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.600
among the first major brokerages to expose live trading endpoints to third-party agent frameworks.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.960
Users should monitor the rollout for custody and approval hyphen flow requirements before

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:42.720
routing capital through automated agents. Work mode in the chat GPT mobile app successfully ran

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:49.260
YT-DLP to pull YouTube subtitles, while chat mode failed on the same command.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:55.140
The difference indicates work mode permits outbound network calls that chat mode blocks.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:01.900
Simon Willison highlighted the split as more significant than the quoting rule confusion he also encountered.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:08.560
The observation emerged while Simon Willison was comparing the two modes on identical code execution

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:15.540
tasks inside the same conversation thread. Developers testing mobile agent prototypes should verify

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.500
which mode their target users have access to before assuming tool use parity.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:27.360
A new analysis walks through recent cases where top models fabricated details and caused downstream

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:33.400
damage. The piece examines both the mechanisms behind hallucinations and mitigation approaches

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:39.140
available today. The author illustrates the point with concrete examples drawn from legal,

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:44.480
medical, and financial domains where fabricated citations produced measurable harm.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.560
Practitioners handling high stakes outputs can use the documented failure modes to build better

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.800
verification layers. The article stresses that even the strongest models still require external

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.900
grounding. The Calibri proof-of-concept shows a frontier-scale model running locally with minimal

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:09.360
RAM. The result is relevant for teams that need to keep data inside corporate boundaries or avoid

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:16.280
API latency. No public code or weights were released with the announcement. Watch for follow-up

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:21.640
open-source ports that would let developers replicate the memory footprint on their own hardware.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:26.980
Okay, let's pop the hood on prompt compression via activation aggregation.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:33.740
Everyone talks about prompt compression as if it simply deletes tokens. In practice, it means deciding

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:39.260
which information must survive inside the model's hidden state rather than in the token stream.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:45.580
The core insight is that a single weighted sum of activations at an intermediate layer can carry

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:51.180
enough task-relevant signal to replace the original prompt tokens when re-injected early in the

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:57.760
network. Because mid-layer representations already encode higher-level semantics, the compressed

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:04.100
vector transfers usefully to earlier layers without retraining the entire stack. The approach

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:10.980
incurs less than 2% accuracy drop on the tasks tested while eliminating the need to reprocess the

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:16.800
full prompt on every query. The quality of the compression depends on choosing the right intermediate

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:23.760
layer and learning stable weights. Too early and the signal is too local. Too late and tasks-specific

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:29.840
details have already been abstracted away. The method also reveals that a single activation vector

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:36.500
can encode a quantifiable amount of recoverable semantic information, a fact confirmed by the author's

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:42.420
reconstruction experiments. Because the compression is learned per instruction, the same vector works

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:48.380
across many future queries that reuse that instruction. This creates a practical cache-like

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:54.860
behavior at the activation level rather than at the token level. Teams should reach for activation

00:08:54.860 --> 00:09:01.320
aggregation when the same instruction is reused across many calls, and latency or context window

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:07.760
pressure is the binding constraint. The main gotcha is that the compression is instruction-specific,

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:14.060
so changing the prompt requires relearning the vector. In production, the technique pairs naturally

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:21.020
with prompt caching systems that already store KV states, offering an orthogonal saving on the prompt

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:31.060
embedding stage itself. Test GPT 5.6 inside Microsoft 365 co-pilot on any existing document or

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:39.100
spreadsheet automation to measure the new cost to quality trade-off. Run the same YT-DLP command

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:45.840
in both chat and work modes of the chat GPT mobile app to map where network tool access is actually

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:53.740
available. Prototype a local colabry style setup on a machine with 32GB of RAM to see whether

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:59.960
